Tool directives written as comments are preamble, not the header: the comment after them is the header, as it is after a shebang or `'use strict'`.
- A `/// <reference />` line doesn't join the `//` comment below it (`reference.ts`); a block directive such as `/* eslint-disable */` ends at its `*/` (`eslint.js`).
- `// @ts-nocheck` comes before a `/** */` header (`nocheck.ts`); several directives in a row are all skipped (`several.js`).
- A file with only directives has no header (`only-directive.ts`), and a comment that merely mentions a directive is an ordinary header (`mention.ts`).
- In CSS, `/* stylelint-disable */` is a directive the same way (`theme.css`).
